What is Cloud Computing?
Cloud computing is the delivery of computing resources such as servers, storage, networking, databases, and applications over the internet.
It allows individuals and organizations to access and use computing resources without owning and managing all of the physical hardware themselves.

Cloud Service Provider
A cloud service provider is a company that provides cloud computing services such as computing power, storage, networking, databases, and software over the internet.

On-Premise vs Cloud Computing
On-Premise Computing
In an on-premise environment, an organization owns or operates its computing infrastructure and is responsible for managing and maintaining the physical systems.

Cloud Computing
In cloud computing, infrastructure and computing services are provided remotely by a cloud service provider.

The amount of responsibility the customer has depends on the type of cloud service being used.




Cloud Service Models
IaaS — Infrastructure as a Service
Infrastructure as a Service provides basic computing resources such as virtual servers, storage, and networking.
The cloud provider manages the underlying physical infrastructure, while the customer is responsible for managing the operating system, applications, and software running on the virtual machines.

Example
You rent a virtual computer, but you manage what runs inside it.



PaaS — Platform as a Service

Platform as a Service provides a managed environment for building and deploying applications.
The cloud provider manages the underlying infrastructure and operating system, allowing developers to focus on building and running their applications.

Example
You focus on building your application without managing the underlying servers.


SaaS — Software as a Service
Software as a Service provides complete software applications over the internet.
Users access the software through a website or application while the provider manages the underlying infrastructure and software.

Examples
Gmail
Zoom
Simple Example

You simply use the finished software.






Cloud Deployment Models
Public Cloud
A public cloud provides computing resources through infrastructure owned and operated by a cloud provider.
Public cloud services are commonly used because they can be:
Cost-effective
Scalable
Flexible

Organizations do not need to own the underlying physical infrastructure.









Private Cloud

A private cloud is a cloud environment dedicated to a single organization.

It can provide:
Greater control
Customization
Support for security and compliance requirements

Private clouds may be useful for organizations handling sensitive information.

Examples include:
Banks
Healthcare organizations
Government organizations







Hybrid Cloud
A hybrid cloud combines private and public cloud environments.
Organizations can keep sensitive workloads or data in a private environment while using public cloud resources when additional scalability is needed.

Example
An e-commerce company may keep sensitive customer information in a private environment while using public cloud resources to handle increased website traffic.











Benefits of Cloud Computing
Scalability
Cloud resources can be scaled up or down depending on the needs of an application.

On-Demand Self-Service
Users can create or remove resources such as servers and storage when needed without purchasing new physical hardware.

Pay for What You Use
Organizations can pay based on the computing resources they consume instead of purchasing all infrastructure upfront.

Security
Cloud providers secure parts of the underlying infrastructure.

However, organizations are still responsible for securing their own accounts, configurations, applications, and data depending on the service model.

High Availability
Cloud infrastructure can be designed with redundancy to help applications remain available even if part of the infrastructure fails.

Global Access
Applications and services can be accessed from different locations through the internet.







